  "title": "Shirts, Shoes and Colours at Toi Market",
  "category": "commerce",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 2,
  "order": 204,
  "theme": "market",
  "culturalNote": "Toi Market and Gikomba are where Nairobi buys its clothes — much of it \"mitumba\", second-hand clothing sold from huge bales. Sellers call out to every passer-by, the first price is always high, and \"bei ya mwisho\" (last price) is part of the game, not the end of it. Colour words for things are mostly n/n: shati nyeupe (white shirt), viatu nyeusi (black shoes). Bluu, kijani and njano never change.",
  "startingPoint": "Saturday at Toi Market. A seller waves a shirt at you from his stall.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "adjective-agreement",
    "numbers-money",
    "present-na",
    "ko-location"
  ],
